minals.\\n\\n- One Voided Suit (1 point): I have tiles in all three suits? W (in meld), T (in hand), B (in hand and meld). So I have all three suits, not voided.\\n\\n- No Honors (1 point): I have no honor tiles in hand or melds? My melds: W3-4-5 (no honors), B2 pung (no honors). Hand: no honors. So I qualify for No Honors (1 point). But that's only 1 point.\\n\\n- Edge Wait/Closed Wait/Single Wait/Self Drawn: not applicable yet.\\n\\n- Chicken Hand (8 points): If my hand would otherwise be worth 0 fans, but with no flower tiles, it's worth 8 points. But I already have some potential points: No Honors (1 point), and if I get All Simples (2 points) by discarding B1, that's 3 points, not 0. So Chicken Hand requires 0 points. So if I avoid any other patterns, I could aim for Chicken Hand? But Chicken Hand is only if the hand has no other scoring patterns. If I have All Simples or No Honors, that's already points, so Chicken Hand wouldn't apply. Chicken Hand is specifically for hands that score 0 points except for the base pattern. So if I have any other pattern, Chicken Hand is not scored. So maybe I should avoid All Simples and No Honors to aim for Chicken Hand? But No Honors is almost unavoidable since I have no honors. Actually, No Honors is a pattern worth 1 point, so if I have it, I cannot have Chicken Hand because Chicken Hand requires the hand to have no other patterns. So if I have No Honors (1 point), I cannot score Chicken Hand. So to get Chicken Hand, I would need to have at least one honor tile in my hand? But I don't have any. Alternatively, I could have a pattern like Melded Kong or something that gives points, but then Chicken Hand not applicable.\\n\\nGiven that, maybe it's easier to aim for a hand that scores at least 8 points through combinations. Let's see possible high-value patterns:\\n- Full Flush (24 points): all tiles in one suit. I have W, T, B tiles, so not possible.\\n- Half Flush (6 points): only one suit and honors. I have multiple suits, so not.\\n- All Pungs (6 points): four pungs/kongs. I have one pung (B2), could try to get three more pungs. My hand has pairs in T3, B4, B8. If I draw third of each, I'd have three more pungs, total four pungs: B2 (exposed), T3, B4, B8 (concealed). That would be All Pungs (6 points) plus maybe Two Concealed Pungs (2 points) for two of them, and possibly Concealed Kong if I get four of something. Also, if any of those pungs are dragons or winds? No, they are all simples. So All Pungs (6) + Two Concealed Pungs (2) = 8 points exactly. That seems achievable. Also, I would have a pair from the remaining tile? In All Pungs pattern, the hand consists of four pungs and a pair. So I need to have four pungs and a pair. If I get pungs of T3, B4, B8, then I have three pungs plus the existing B2 pung, that's four pungs. Then I need a pair. Currently, after using T3, B4, B8 as pungs, I would have used all my hand tiles? Let's simulate: Hand tiles: T2, T3, T3, B1, B4, B4, B8, B8. If I draw a third T3, I can form a T3 pung (using two T3 and the new one). Then I have left: T2, B1, B4, B4, B8, B8. If I draw a third B4, form B4 pung, left: T2, B1, B8, B8. If I draw a third B8, form B8 pung, left: T2, B1. Then I need a pair from T2 and B1? They are different, so I need to draw either another T2 or another B1 to make a pair. So I need four specific draws: T3, B4, B8, and either T2 or B1. That's quite difficult.\\n\\nAlternatively, I could use one of the pairs as the final pair, and only need two more pungs. For example, keep B8 pair as the final pair, and get pungs for T3 and B4. Then I need: draw T3 and B4, and then I have hand: after forming T3 pung and B4 pung, I have left: T2, B1, B8, B8. That's T2, B1, and B8 pair. So I need to get rid of T2 and B1 or make them into a meld. I could try to form chows with them, but they are not adjacent. So maybe discard T2 and B1, and keep B8 pair as final pair. But then I need to draw the pungs. So still need two specific draws.\\n\\nMaybe another pattern: Seven Pairs (24 points). I currently have three pairs in hand: T3, B4, B8. Plus I have a pair? Actually, I have exactly three pairs: T3 (2 tiles), B4 (2), B8 (2). And two singles: T2, B1. So I need four more pairs to make seven pairs. That would require drawing four more pairs, which is even harder.\\n\\nGiven the difficulty, I should probably aim for a simpler hand like All Simples + some other patterns. But All Simples is only 2 points, No Honors is 1 point, maybe Self Drawn 1 point, etc. That's not enough. I need at least 8.\\n\\nObserving the game history: I have done a Chi and a Peng. So my hand is not concealed. That limits some patterns like Concealed Hand.\\n\\nMaybe I should consider discarding the terminal B1 to move towards All Simples and then try to complete a hand with pungs/chows that give additional points. But All Simples alone is not enough.\\n\\nLet's look at the valid actions: I can play B4, T2, B1, T3, B8. I need to choose which to discard.\\n\\nFactors: \\n- B1 is terminal, discarding it removes a terminal, helping All Simples.\\n- T2 is simple, but it might be useful for chows with T3.\\n- T3 is a pair, discarding one breaks the pair.\\n- B4 and B8 are pairs.\\n\\nTo maximize potential for All Pungs, I should keep pairs. So discard singles: T2 or B1. Between T2 and B1, B1 is terminal and less useful because terminal chows require 1-2-3 or 7-8-9, and I have no adjacent tiles. T2 can combine with T3 to form part of a Chow if I get T1 or T4. But T1 is terminal, T4 is simple. T4 is more available (3 left). So T2 might be more useful for a Chow than B1. Also, discarding B1 reduces terminals, which is good for All Simples. But if I keep B1, I could aim for Pung of Terminals (1 point) if I get two more B1. But B1 only has 2 left, so unlikely.\\n\\nAlso, from the unshown tiles, B1 has 2 left, T2 has 3 left, T3 has 3 left, B4 has 3 left, B8 has 3 left.\\n\\nI also need to consider what other players have. Next player has melds: F3 pung and W1-2-3 chow. Across has T1-2-3 chow and T9 pung. Previous player has no melds. So terminals and honors are being used. F3 is out (0 left). T9 has 1 left. etc.\\n\\nGiven the state, I think the best plan is to aim for a hand that scores at least 8 points through combinations like All Pungs + Two Concealed Pungs, or maybe Mixed Triple Chow, etc. But given my tiles, All Pungs seems plausible if I draw well.\\n\\nFor now, I should discard a tile that is less useful for forming pungs or chows. B1 seems the weakest because it's a terminal with no adjacent tiles in my hand. T2 at least has T3 adjacent. So discard B1.\\n\\nBut let's check if discarding B1 might help opponents? B1 is terminal, but across player has T1-2-3 chow, so maybe they need B1 for something? Unlikely. Previous player's history shows they played B8, B2, etc. Not sure.\\n\\nAlso, from my history, I did Chi W4 and Peng B2. So I have claimed tiles.\\n\\nNow, to output:\\n\\nEvaluation: Discarding B1 is preferable as it is a terminal tile with low potential for melds in my current hand, removes a terminal to possibly aim for All Simples, and retains pairs for potential pungs.\\n\\nReason: B1 is a terminal with no adjacent tiles in hand, making it difficult to form chows. Keeping T2 offers more flexibility with T3 for possible chows. Maintaining pairs of T3, B4, B8 is important for potential pungs to score All Pungs and Two Concealed Pungs, which could reach 8 points.\\n\\nAnswer: Play B1.\"}"}
