t T3 gone, not possible.  \nSo waits: B6 or B9 only. Also if draw B8 \u2192 B8 pair + meld? Meld from what? We\u2019d have B8\u00d73 and B7, T2; not complete unless T2 pairs or B7 pairs.  \n\nBetter discard: **T2** \u2192 Keep T3,B7,B8,B8.  \nWaits:  \n- Chow B6,B7,B8 (B6)  \n- Chow B7,B8,B9 (B9)  \n- Chow T2,T3,T4 (needs T2,T4) \u2192 not possible (T2 gone)  \n- Chow T1,T2,T3 (needs T1,T2) not possible.  \nSo same as discarding T3.  \n\nBest is discard **B8** \u2192 Keep B7,B8,T2,T3.  \nWaits:  \n- Chow B6,B7,B8 (B6)  \n- Chow B7,B8,B9 (B9)  \n- Chow T2,T3,T4 (T4)  \n- Chow T1,T2,T3 (T1)  \nThat\u2019s 4 different chow possibilities. Unshown tiles: T4(3), T1(1), B6(3), B9(4). Total 11 tiles in 34 unknown tiles.  \n\nSo discarding **B8** gives more waiting tiles, thus higher winning chance.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding one B8 maximizes possible chow completions, offering more tile draws to finish the hand. It retains flexibility in both suits (Bamboo and Characters). The hand already has exposed melds in three suits, so completing a Chow in either Bamboo or Characters will yield a valid winning hand with All Types pattern possible (if final tile is an honor) or at least basic hand.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B8"}
